<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="connect-multiple-accounts"
      updated="August 22, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"binding", "One connection, one account"},
        {"servers", "One MCP server entry per account"},
        {"cli", "The CLI: stored accounts"},
        {"cloud", "Cloud connectors: one per account"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="connect-multiple-accounts">
        <:dek>
          You can belong to several accounts — production and staging, or a client's account
          beside your own. A connection binds one account, so working across several means one
          connection per account, named so you and the model can always tell them apart.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_prerequisites>
        <:item>Membership in each account you want to connect.</:item>
        <:item>
          For key-based connections, permission to mint an agent key in each account — any
          member can mint their own.
        </:item>
      </.docs_prerequisites>

      <.docs_h2 id="binding">One connection, one account</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        An
        <.docs_inline_code>emk-</.docs_inline_code>
        key belongs to the account that minted it, and a cloud connector's authorization is
        granted for one account you choose in the browser. A connection does not switch
        accounts mid-conversation — the account is decided when you wire it up, which is what
        keeps every run and audit event attributed to the right place.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="servers">One MCP server entry per account</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        For a client that runs the
        <.docs_inline_code>emisar-mcp</.docs_inline_code>
        bridge — Claude Code, Cursor, and the other local clients — mint a key in each
        account's <strong>AI agents</strong>
        page, then add one server entry per account.
        Put the account in the entry's name: the client namespaces tool calls by that name,
        so "check staging" has an unambiguous target and the model cannot mix accounts up.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="mcpServers — one entry per account">{~s({
  "mcpServers": {
    "emisar-prod": {
      "command": "emisar-mcp",
      "env": {
        "EMISAR_URL": "https://emisar.dev",
        "EMISAR_API_KEY": "emk-…the prod account's key…"
      }
    },
    "emisar-staging": {
      "command": "emisar-mcp",
      "env": {
        "EMISAR_URL": "https://emisar.dev",
        "EMISAR_API_KEY": "emk-…the staging account's key…"
      }
    }
  }
})}</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The snippet shape and each client's config location are in
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/connect-cli-agent"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Connect a CLI agent</.link>
        — the console generates a ready snippet per client, and a
        client registered with
        <.docs_inline_code>claude mcp add</.docs_inline_code>
        takes a second registration under the other name.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="cli">The CLI: stored accounts</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The bridge is also a shell CLI, and it holds credentials for several accounts at once.
        Run
        <.docs_inline_code>emisar-mcp auth</.docs_inline_code>
        once per account — it opens the browser, you choose the account, and the credential is
        stored locally. Then:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell">emisar-mcp accounts list
<span class="text-zinc-500">CURRENT  ACCOUNT         SLUG  ENDPOINT
*        Northstar Labs  demo  https://emisar.dev
         Acme Logistics  acme  https://emisar.dev</span></.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The star marks the current account — the one commands use when you name none. Switch
        it, or point a single command somewhere else without switching:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell">emisar-mcp accounts use acme
<span class="text-zinc-500">Using Acme Logistics (acme) at https://emisar.dev</span></.docs_code>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell">emisar-mcp --account demo recent_runs</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <.docs_inline_code>--account</.docs_inline_code>
        is how an agent works across accounts from one shell: tell it which account each
        command targets, and the audit trail in each account shows exactly what ran there.
        When
        <.docs_inline_code>EMISAR_URL</.docs_inline_code>
        and
        <.docs_inline_code>EMISAR_API_KEY</.docs_inline_code>
        are both set, that pair wins — direct commands use it, and
        <.docs_inline_code>--account</.docs_inline_code>
        is refused until you unset both. The complete command surface is in <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/mcp-reference"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >MCP CLI &amp; reference</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="cloud">Cloud connectors: one per account</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Claude.ai and ChatGPT hold one authorization per connector, and the authorization is
        granted for one account you choose on the consent screen. To connect multiple
        accounts, add a connector for each one and name it for its account. You sign in with
        the same email each time. The walkthroughs are in
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/connect-claude-ai"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Connect Claude.ai</.link>
        and <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/connect-chatgpt"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Connect ChatGPT</.link>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Every key and connector appears on that account's <strong>AI agents</strong>
        page under the member who created it, with its own activity, rotation, and revoke —
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/agents-and-keys"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Manage agents &amp; keys</.link>
        covers the lifecycle.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
